INT. FARMHOUSE - SPARE BEDROOM - NIGHT

A single bed. An oil lamp. Cold wind knocks at the window. A wardrobe stands half-open.

ESTHER VANCE, 60s, a widow in a quilted robe, perches on a hard chair with her hands knotted. Across the room, THE LODGER, 30s, sits on the edge of the bed, pale, shoes still on, his coat soaked.

LODGER
Thank you for the room.

ESTHER
Rent is due Friday.

LODGER
I'll pay.

ESTHER
I'm sure.

A floorboard creaks in the hall. Neither looks.

ESTHER (CONT'D)
I don't recall your name.

LODGER
It doesn't matter.

ESTHER
It matters to me.

The wind rattles the glass. The lamp gutters. He lifts his head. His eyes catch the flame in two small points.

LODGER
Calloway.

ESTHER
Mr. Calloway.

CALLOWAY
I didn't think I'd be asked.

ESTHER
Most guests give it freely.

CALLOWAY
I'm not most guests.

ESTHER
CALLOWAY
(speaking at once)
No. I see that.
(speaking at once)
You see more than you should.

She rises. The chair scrapes. She crosses to the wardrobe and closes it, softly, with a click.

ESTHER (CONT'D)
Good night, Mr. Calloway.

CALLOWAY
Good night.

She leaves. The door shuts. He sits a while in the lamplight. Then he rises, goes to the wardrobe, and opens it again.
